C.2 The half-turn operator on affine functions This subsection establishes a conditional half-turn estimate for affine observables. After rescaling X-y by the Gaussian scale \sqrt{\eta} , we show that the half-turn nearly maps every affine function with mean zero to its negative. The proof combines a Duhamel representation of the half-turn with covariance identities for the rescaled conditional law. In Section C.3 , this estimate will be applied to the conditional affine part of \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w in the proof of Proposition B.2 . We first fix y\in\mathbb{R}^{d} and work under the conditional law of X given Y=y . The bounds below are uniform in y and will later be integrated over y . To put the Gaussian confinement and harmonic dynamics on unit scale, set W\coloneqq\frac{X-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptq_{y}\coloneqq\Law(W\mid Y=y)\,. The density of q_{y} and the scaled potential gradient are q_{y}(\mathrm{d}w)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V(y+\sqrt{\eta}w)-\frac{1}{2}\|w\|^{2}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}w\,,\hskip 20.00003ptg_{y}(w)\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(y+\sqrt{\eta}w)\,. Thus, the negative log-density of q_{y} has Hessian I+\nabla g_{y} , and \nabla\log q_{y}(w)=-(w+g_{y}(w))\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\alpha\eta I\preceq\nabla g_{y}(w)\preceq\beta\eta I\,. (C.10) In particular, q_{y} is 1 -strongly log-concave. We use the same symbol \mathsf{H}_{y} for the conditional half-turn operator in the normalized coordinate w=(x-y)/\sqrt{\eta} : (\mathsf{H}_{y}f)(w)\coloneqq\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f\Bigl(\frac{x^{\prime}-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}\Bigr)\,\mathcal{H}_{y}(y+\sqrt{\eta}w,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\,. By Proposition 3.2 , \mathsf{H}_{y} is a self-adjoint Markov contraction on L^{2}(q_{y}) . Put \bar{w}_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}W\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\Sigma_{y}\coloneqq\mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(W)\,. Thus, \Sigma_{y} is the conditional covariance in the normalized coordinate; equivalently, we have the identity \mathrm{Cov}(X\mid Y=y)=\eta\Sigma_{y} . Accordingly, I-\Sigma_{y}=\eta\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)\,, by ( D.10 ). Thus I-\Sigma_{y} is both the covariance defect relative to a standard Gaussian and the normalized curvature of the Y -marginal. For a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , write \ell_{a,y}:w\mapsto a^{\top}(w-\bar{w}_{y}) ; this affine function has mean zero under q_{y} . The main result of this subsection is the following estimate. Proposition C.2 (Weighted half-turn estimate for affine functions) . Assume \beta\eta\leq 1/2 . Uniformly in y , every \ell_{a,y}:w\mapsto a^{\top}(w-\bar{w}_{y}) satisfies \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{y})\ell_{a,y}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,a^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a\,. (C.11) More generally, suppose that y\mapsto a_{y} is measurable and \int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)<\infty , and define \ell(x,y)\coloneqq a_{y}^{\top}\Bigl(\frac{x-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}-\bar{w}_{y}\Bigr)\,. Then, \ell\in\mathfrak{H}_{\perp} and \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\ell\|^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. We prove the proposition after two preparatory results. The first gives a Duhamel representation of the deviation from exact sign reversal; the second bounds the resulting term in terms of I-\Sigma_{y} . To analyze \mathsf{H}_{y}\ell_{a,y} , first fix a reference point \widetilde{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} in the rescaled coordinate. The resulting phase-space process is \begin{gathered}\dot{w}=p\,,\qquad\dot{p}=-\{w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}\,,\\ \text{event rate }[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}\,,\\ p\longmapsto R_{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})}p\quad\text{at a bounce}\,.\end{gathered} The construction and path reversal arguments of Appendix A apply verbatim, with phase space law q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . Denote the resulting strongly continuous contraction semigroup by (\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}})_{t\geq 0} . On smooth compactly supported observables, its generator is \begin{split}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}f(w,p)&\coloneqq p^{\top}\nabla_{w}f(w,p)-\{w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}^{\top}\nabla_{p}f(w,p)\\ &\qquad{}+[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}\bigl\{f(w,R_{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})}p)-f(w,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split} The operator \mathsf{H}_{y} is recovered by drawing \widetilde{W}\sim q_{y} , running the corresponding process for time \pi , and averaging over \widetilde{W} and the initial Gaussian momentum. If g_{y}\equiv 0 , then q_{y}=\mathcal{N}(0,I) , \bar{w}_{y}=0 , and the bounce rate vanishes. The dynamics reduce to harmonic motion, w_{t}=w_{0}\cos t+p_{0}\sin t\,,\hskip 20.00003ptp_{t}=-w_{0}\sin t+p_{0}\cos t\,, so \ell_{a,y}(w_{\pi})=-\ell_{a,y}(w_{0}) . For general g_{y} , the discrepancy between the harmonic center -g_{y}(\widetilde{w}) and the conditional mean \bar{w}_{y} , together with the residual gradient bounces, produces an inhomogeneous term. The next lemma identifies this term. Lemma C.3 (Affine Duhamel formula) . For a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} and \widetilde{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , set k_{a,\widetilde{w}}(w,p)\coloneqq-a^{\top}\{\bar{w}_{y}+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}-2\,[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}^{2}\,\frac{a^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}}{\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}}\,, where the quotient is defined as zero when g_{y}(w)=g_{y}(\widetilde{w}) . Then k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\in L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) , and for every t\geq 0 , \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}=\ell_{a,y}\cos t+a^{\top}p\sin t+\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin(t-s)\,\mathrm{d}s in L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . In particular, (I+\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{w}})\ell_{a,y}=\int_{0}^{\pi}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin s\,\mathrm{d}s\,. (C.12) Proof. The generator maps the position observable \ell_{a,y} to the momentum observable a^{\top}p , and maps a^{\top}p back to -\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}} . Thus, this pair evolves as a harmonic system with the additional term k_{a,\widetilde{w}} , and variation of constants gives the claimed formula. We first establish the required L^{2} bounds and justify these generator identities for the unbounded observables. First, k_{a,\widetilde{w}} is square integrable. Its first term is constant in (w,p) , while the absolute value of its second term is at most \displaystyle 2\,[p^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}]_{+}^{2}\,\frac{|a^{\top}\{g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\}|}{\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}} \displaystyle\leq 2\,\|a\|\,\|p\|^{2}\,\|g_{y}(w)-g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\| \displaystyle\leq 2\beta\eta\,\|a\|\,\|p\|^{2}\,\|w-\widetilde{w}\|\,. Since q_{y} is 1 -strongly log-concave and the momentum is Gaussian, this bound shows that k_{a,\widetilde{w}} belongs to L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . The same moment bounds give \ell_{a,y},a^{\top}p\in L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)) . Using a^{\top}(R_{h}p-p)=-2(p^{\top}h)(a^{\top}h)/\|h\|^{2} and [u]_{+}u=[u]_{+}^{2} , the generator formula gives \displaystyle\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y} \displaystyle=a^{\top}p\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)=-\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\,. To justify these identities for the unbounded observables, set H_{\widetilde{w}}(w,p)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\,\{\|w+g_{y}(\widetilde{w})\|^{2}+\|p\|^{2}\} , which is preserved by both the flow and the bounces. Let \chi_{R} be a smooth cutoff equal to one on [0,R] and zero on [2R,\infty) . Conservation of H_{\widetilde{w}} gives, for F\in\{\ell_{a,y},a^{\top}p\} , \mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}\{\chi_{R}(H_{\widetilde{w}})F\}=\chi_{R}(H_{\widetilde{w}})\,\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{w}}F . Applying Dynkin’s formula and then letting R\to\infty in L^{2} , using the preceding integrability bounds and semigroup contractivity, yields \displaystyle\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}-\ell_{a,y} \displaystyle=\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)\,\mathrm{d}s\,, \displaystyle\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(a^{\top}p)-a^{\top}p \displaystyle=\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}(-\ell_{a,y}+k_{a,\widetilde{w}})\,\mathrm{d}s\,. Applying variation of constants to this integral system gives \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}}\ell_{a,y}=\ell_{a,y}\cos t+a^{\top}p\sin t+\int_{0}^{t}\mathsf{T}_{s}^{y,\widetilde{w}}k_{a,\widetilde{w}}\sin(t-s)\,\mathrm{d}s\,. This is the first identity in the lemma. At t=\pi , the homogeneous part is -\ell_{a,y} and \sin(\pi-s)=\sin s , which gives ( C.12 ). ∎ The proof of Proposition C.2 will also use the following identities and bounds. Lemma C.4 (Conditional gradient covariance bound) . Fix y and assume \beta\eta\leq 1/2 . Then \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y} , and \mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(W,g_{y}(W))=I-\Sigma_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathrm{Cov}_{q_{y}}(g_{y}(W))\preceq\frac{\beta\eta}{1-\beta\eta}\,(I-\Sigma_{y})\preceq 2\beta\eta\,(I-\Sigma_{y})\,. (C.13) Proof. Under Y_{+}=y , we have X=y+\sqrt{\eta}W and Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}W . Applying ( C.9 ) to f:u\mapsto F((u-y)/(2\sqrt{\eta})) therefore gives \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}\nabla F(W)=\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}[F(W)\,\{W+g_{y}(W)\}]\,. Taking F\equiv 1 and then the coordinate functions of W and g_{y}(W) , with the same cutoff approximation as in Lemma C.1 , gives \mathbb{E}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathrm{Cov}(W,g_{y}(W))=I-\Sigma_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}\nabla g_{y}(W)=I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\,. For every a\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , the same conditional Poincaré estimate used in the proof of Lemma C.1 , together with (\nabla g_{y})^{2}\preceq\beta\eta\,\nabla g_{y} then give \displaystyle a^{\top}\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))a \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}\|\nabla g_{y}(W)\,a\|^{2}\leq\beta\eta\,a^{\top}\{I-\Sigma_{y}+\mathrm{Cov}(g_{y}(W))\}a\,. Rearranging proves ( C.13 ). ∎ Proof of Proposition C.2 . Fix y , and let \widetilde{W}\sim q_{y} be independent of (W,P)\sim q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I) . We first apply the Duhamel formula to identify the estimate that remains to be proved. Using, in order, Jensen’s inequality in \widetilde{W} , ( C.12 ), the Bochner triangle inequality, the contractivity of Gaussian momentum averaging and \mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{w}} , and \int_{0}^{\pi}\sin t\,\mathrm{d}t=2 , we obtain \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{y})\ell_{a,y}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2} \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{W}}\bigl\|\mathbb{E}_{P}[(I+\mathsf{T}_{\pi}^{y,\widetilde{W}})\ell_{a,y}]\bigr\|_{L^{2}(q_{y})}^{2} \displaystyle\leq\mathbb{E}_{\widetilde{W}}\Bigl(\int_{0}^{\pi}\|\mathsf{T}_{t}^{y,\widetilde{W}}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\|_{L^{2}(q_{y}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I))}\sin t\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr)^{2}\leq 4\,\mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}\,. It remains to bound the second moment on the right. Conditional on (W,\widetilde{W}) , a direct calculation with the Gaussian momentum P , together with \mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)=-\bar{w}_{y} from Lemma C.4 , gives \displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{P}[k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\mid W,\widetilde{W}] \displaystyle=-a^{\top}\{g_{y}(W)-\mathbb{E}_{q_{y}}g_{y}(W)\}\,, \displaystyle\Var_{P}(k_{a,\widetilde{W}}\mid W,\widetilde{W}) \displaystyle=5\,[a^{\top}\{g_{y}(W)-g_{y}(\widetilde{W})\}]^{2}\,. After averaging over the independent variables W and \widetilde{W} , the conditional variance term contributes 10\Var_{q_{y}}(a^{\top}g_{y}(W)) , while the squared conditional mean contributes one more copy of the same variance. Consequently, \mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}=11\Var_{q_{y}}(a^{\top}g_{y}(W))\,. By Lemma C.4 , \mathbb{E}_{W,\widetilde{W},P}k_{a,\widetilde{W}}^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,a^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a\,. Combining the last estimate with the Duhamel bound proves ( C.11 ). For the global assertion, Proposition 2.1 gives I-\Sigma_{y}\succeq\alpha\eta\,(1+\alpha\eta)^{-1}\,I , so the stated integrability condition implies \ell\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) . Its conditional mean is zero. Applying the conditional inequality with a=a_{y} and integrating in y proves the result. ∎